Creating New Launch Products

Purpose
- This guide defines how to prompt and build future launch products that rely on go4launch/index.html logic without causing expiration conflicts.
- Core mission rule: launch cards must remain visible for 48 hours after a launch transitions from GO/IN FLIGHT into a completed state.
- A passed NET does not mean liftoff. Delayed launches must remain active until LL2 marks them in flight/completed or publishes a later NET.

Non-Negotiable Product Rules
1) Preserve lifecycle visibility:
   - Future launch (NET <= future horizon): show card.
   - Delayed-but-not-completed launch (NET < now, status not 3/4/6/7): keep showing the card and treat it as awaiting an update, not as liftoff.
   - In-flight (status id 6): always show card.
   - Completed (status id 3, 4, or 7): keep card for 48 hours after NET.
2) Never use a pure NET cutoff such as "net >= now" as the only filter.
3) Never use "exclude completed" logic by itself.
4) Never change countdown text to "Liftoff" or "Launched" only because the scheduled NET passed.
   - Only show liftoff/launched language when LL2 status indicates in-flight or a completed outcome.
   - If NET has passed and status is still pending/hold/TBD/TBC/GO, show an "awaiting update" or hold message instead.
5) Keep Launch Library endpoint usage consistent:
   - Use LL2 base host: https://ll.thespacedevs.com/2.3.0
   - List endpoints must be singular:
     - /launch/upcoming/
     - /launch/previous/
   - Single-launch lookups must be singular:
     - /launch/{id}/
   - Never use deprecated/mismatched forms such as lldev.thespacedevs.com or /launches/{id}/.
   - Merge and deduplicate by launch id.
6) Fallback behavior must mirror backend behavior, not a reduced dataset.
7) Open products must refresh launch data on a short loop so delays are picked up while the screen stays open.
   - Countdown-centric screens: about every 1 minute.
   - General launch boards/cards: about every 5 minutes.

Prompting Template for New Launch Products
When requesting a new launch display, include this exact intent:

"Build this product using the same launch lifecycle logic as go4launch/index.html.
Cards must display for:
- all future launches,
- delayed launches whose NET has passed but whose status is not yet in-flight or completed,
- all in-flight launches (status id 6),
- completed launches (status ids 3/4/7) for exactly 48 hours after NET.
Do not filter using only NET >= now, do not equate passed NET with liftoff, and do not remove completed launches immediately.
If using LL2 fallback, use https://ll.thespacedevs.com/2.3.0 and fetch both /launch/upcoming/ and /launch/previous/, merge, dedupe by id, then apply lifecycle filtering.
If a detail page performs direct LL2 lookup, use /launch/{id}/ (never /launches/{id}/).
If the screen remains open, refresh launch data on a recurring timer so delay updates can replace stale NET values."

Required Acceptance Checks
- A launch at T+1 minute with status 6 is still visible.
- A launch at T+1 minute with status 5 or 2 is still visible and is not labeled liftoff.
- A launch at T+2 hours with status 3 is still visible.
- A launch at T+47 hours with status 4 is still visible.
- A launch at T+49 hours with status 3 is no longer visible.
- If backend data fails, fallback still shows in-flight and recent completed launches.
- If a screen stays open through a delay, the displayed launch time/status updates after the next refresh cycle.

Conflict Prevention for Future Products
- Any new go4launch-derived product must implement lifecycle filtering first, then visual sorting.
- Filtering logic should be centralized in a dedicated helper (for example: shouldShowLaunch).
- Do not create separate ad hoc filters for different panels/cards that can drift from mission rules.
- Re-check any periodic refresh logic to ensure status transitions do not immediately drop cards.
- Any countdown component should have a dedicated helper for expired-NET behavior so delayed launches do not display false liftoff text.

Operational Note
- Channel playlist expiration logic is separate from go4launch card lifecycle filtering.
- Product card visibility must be controlled by lifecycle/status logic in the launch product itself.
